### Event-System

**Verwendetes System:** C# Events mit `Action` und `Action<T>`

**Standard-Pattern:**
```csharp
// In der Klasse die Event feuert
public class HotBarManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    // Event deklarieren
    public event Action<int> OnHotBarSelectionChanged;
    
    public void SelectSlot(int index)
    {
        currentSelectedSlot = index;
        
        // Event feuern (null-check für safety)
        OnHotBarSelectionChanged?.Invoke(index);
    }
}

// In der Klasse die reagiert
public class InventoryBar : MonoBehaviour
{
    private HotBarManager hotBarManager;
    
    void Start()
    {
        hotBarManager = HotBarManager.Instance;
        
        // Event abonnieren
        hotBarManager.OnHotBarSelectionChanged += HandleSelectionChanged;
    }
    
    void OnDestroy()
    {
        // WICHTIG: Event abmelden um Memory Leaks zu vermeiden
        if (hotBarManager != null)
        {
            hotBarManager.OnHotBarSelectionChanged -= HandleSelectionChanged;
        }
    }
    
    private void HandleSelectionChanged(int newIndex)
    {
        // Reagiere auf Event
        UpdateSelectionHighlight(newIndex);
    }
}
```

**Beispiel-Events im Projekt:**
```csharp
// HotBarManager
public event Action<int> OnHotBarSelectionChanged;

// InventoryManager
public event Action OnInventoryChanged;

// InputService (vermutlich)
public event Action<Vector2> OnMovePerformed;
public event Action OnInteractPerformed;
public event Action OnUseToolPerformed;
public event Action<int> OnHotBarSlotPressed;
```

**Best Practices:**
- Event-Namen beginnen mit `On` (z.B. `OnInventoryChanged`)
- Immer `?.Invoke()` nutzen (null-safe)
- **KRITISCH:** Events in `OnDestroy()` abmelden!
- Für komplexe Event-Daten: Custom EventArgs nutzen

